@node Terminology
@chapter Terminology

@table @dfn
@item dual-role key
A physical key configured with a @dfn{hold key} and a @dfn{tap key}.
@item hold key
The key a dual-role key emits while it is held, for example Left Control.
@item tap key
The key a dual-role key emits when it is tapped, for example Escape.
@item remap
A one-to-one mapping in which every event of a source key is emitted as the
same event of a target key.
@item managed key
A key named in the active configuration as a dual-role key or a remap source.
@item protected key
A key the engine leaves unmapped regardless of the configuration.
@item tap timeout
The longest interval between the press and the release of a dual-role key
for which that release still counts as a tap.
@item used
A dual-role key is @dfn{used} once any other key press, or any pointer button
press, arrives between its press and its release.
@item engine
The @code{escapepod-core} state machine.
@item held-keys ledger
The record of every key Escape Pod holds down on the virtual device, and
which device holds it.
@item daemon
A running @code{escapepod daemon run} process.
@item evdev backend
The daemon mode that grabs kernel input devices and writes to a uinput
virtual device.
@item X11 backend
The daemon mode that observes keys through the X RECORD extension and
injects tap keys through the XTEST extension.
@item agent environment
A process environment in which @env{AI_AGENT} or @env{AGENT} is set to a
non-empty value, or @env{CI} is set to a value other than @samp{0} or
@samp{false}.
@item emergency chord
A set of physical keys which, held together, makes the daemon release every
grab and exit.
@item reference machine
The machine budgets are measured on (§22.4): a Lenovo ThinkPad T490s
(Intel Core i7-8665U, 4 cores and 8 threads, 32 GiB of RAM, NVMe SSD,
NixOS 26.05), with the daemon pinned to two cores so that it stands in for
the low-end baseline of a 2-core x86-64 machine with 4 GiB of RAM.  The
reference workload is defined under @ref{Budgets}.
@end table

@node Needs
@chapter Needs

@table @asis
@item ESC-NEED-001 @emph{One key, two jobs}
A Vim user who has moved Control to the Caps Lock position wants that key to
send Escape when tapped and act as Control when held, so that both stay under
the left little finger.

@item ESC-NEED-002 @emph{Every session}
The behaviour works the same under X11, under Wayland compositors, and on the
Linux console, and still works in an X11 session where the user cannot be
given access to kernel input devices.

@item ESC-NEED-003 @emph{Never stranded}
A failure, crash, misconfiguration, or reload never leaves the user without a
working keyboard, and a stuck modifier never outlives the process.

@item ESC-NEED-004 @emph{Keystrokes stay private}
Installing Escape Pod does not give the user's login account, the user's other
programs, or an AI agent working for the user any ability to read keystrokes
that they did not have before.

@item ESC-NEED-005 @emph{No perceptible delay}
Typing through Escape Pod is indistinguishable from typing without it, and an
idle daemon costs no power.

@item ESC-NEED-006 @emph{Operable by people and agents}
The tool is configured, inspected, and diagnosed from the command line by a
person at a terminal or by an AI agent, under the Spacecraft Software CLI
Standard.

@item ESC-NEED-007 @emph{Assistive technology first}
Escape Pod never takes a key away from a screen reader that relies on it.

@item ESC-NEED-008 @emph{xcape users can move over}
A user of xcape can switch with the mapping string they already use.

@item ESC-NEED-009 @emph{Mouse shortcuts}
A user who sends desktop shortcuts from extra mouse buttons, such as the
side buttons switching workspaces, can do so with Escape Pod alone, so no
second remapper competes with it for the same devices.
@end table

Constraints the maintainer will not trade away: the scope stays a dual-role
and one-to-one remapper, plus one fixed shortcut per mouse button (no
layers, macros, or keyboard chords); tapping is
pass-through (the hold key is emitted at once, never buffered); the platform
is Linux; the language is Rust.

